## ===========================================================================
## THE NIGHT JOURNEY TO LORD'S KEEP — Book 5B (`story.ni:6550-7596`),
## Chapter 4.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 4 — The Night Journey to Lord's Keep",
## 2026-09-01) authorizes Book 5B whole: the night walk from behind Maiden
## House to the empty market, Bobby under the post, Lord's Road, the Pasture,
## the Stream Crossing, the Woods and the split sapling, the Clearing and the
## fountain, the tunnel, the loose brick and the wall, the Chapel, the two
## baileys, the Guardhouse and the two listens at the arrow slit, then the
## way back to the capture in the Clearing and the sack. Every line is
## Gentry's; conversations are STUBS under the standing rule.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 4):
##   * THE RAILS — "open outside the keep, with a chase for dawdling". The
##     nerve refusals on the Market Square's east exit, Lord's Road's south,
##     the Pasture's west and the Crossing's south are NOT carried; Jack can
##     wander and Bobby waits at the next stage. The Southern Gate stays
##     blocked ("guarded day and night", `story.ni:10982`); the Woods keep
##     the sapling; inside the tunnels and the keep the rail is the source's,
##     gestures and all.
##   * THE DAWDLE CHASE — new content, a scripted beat not a fail state. A
##     four-turn clock on the player (`dawdle`) starts when Bobby heads north
##     and restarts on the first arrival at each stage outside the keep
##     (Lord's Road, the Pasture, the Crossing); it stops in the Woods. When
##     it expires, Bobby comes to wherever she is, the hide beat plays as ONE
##     placeholder (the chase, Bobby catching up, the hiding as they pass —
##     David's lines), and Bobby goes back to the stage ahead of her. It
##     fires at most once: an expired clock is never restarted. MEASURED AND
##     REPORTED, not decided: four free turns per stage, one clock, re-armed
##     per stage — the gap the change document left to this build.
##   * THE STREET PUSH — new content, fear without teeth. ONE placeholder on
##     the walk, on Commerce Street's first entry at night (the last stretch
##     before the market); the beats and their lines are David's.
##   * WHO SEES HER — Bobby does not; the guards behind the slit are public
##     tier ("yer urchin boy" carries); the gaunt man in red robes SEES — his
##     "young...sir? Or is it 'miss'?" is a tell, carried as written for David
##     to keep or sharpen (## DAVID: below).
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## `talk to bobby` in the Market Square prints the BO23-BO26 TODO and takes
## the exit — Bobby heads north to Lord's Road and the clock starts; walking
## north without talking does the same through the source's own walk-past
## text. `talk to bobby` in the Clearing prints the BO27-BO31 TODO and takes
## BO31's side effect — down the hole.
##
## HOW BOBBY IS AHEAD. Each stage's first arrival moves him to the next room
## and speaks his entry text (the source's `After going` rules); the Woods
## send him straight to the Clearing (the source removes him from play and
## re-places him there — here he waits in the Clearing, so `follow` and
## `Bobby's location` always resolve). `following` is a story action with a
## topic slot: Bobby here refuses; Bobby ahead moves her to his room (an
## action's refusals are gates checked before its body, whatever the
## textual order — so the gates carry the exceptions). On the
## way back a region daemon brings him along ("Bobby follows you"), guarded
## on the player being in the region — a region daemon fires wherever the
## player is (probed 2026-09-04; the guide says otherwise).
##
## PRESENCE LINES. A `phrase present:` cannot vary by condition (markers are
## refused inside a phrase body, and a marker in an entity's description
## prints literally — both probed 2026-09-04), so Bobby carries NO present
## line: each room's description carries a gated marker instead
## (`{bobby-waiting}` while he is here; the alley and the market have their
## own lines). The fountain's initial appearance is the Clearing's marker
## for the same reason.
##
## Not carried, noted: the facing texts (peering not extended, as Chapters 2
## and 3); the source's plinth/base aliases moving between the statue and
## the entrance (the entrance owns them); `rub`/`extinguish`/`burn` (no
## actions); the bare `listen` in the Guardhouse as a listen at the slit
## (the slit's two-row count lives on the slit); the hole, brick and lever
## behind the wall (Pieter's chapter, offstage until then in the source);
## the dried leaves as an entity (no description in the source); `taste`
## (no standard action consults tasting); `pause the
## game` before the chapter card; the night sky, moon and stars (no backdrop
## in the port); the Southern Gate as a room (Chapter 10's). Glue text of
## mine, not Gentry's, marked GLUE in comments.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CLOCK — the dawdle (change document, "The rails")
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define timer dawdle for the player
  lingering
  dawdling
  loitering
  lagging
end timer

