## THE WOODS and the split sapling (`story.ni:6872-6960`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The sapling's way is drawn once, on the first arrival, from the story's
## seeded stream (deterministic at the pinned seed, as the sweep is): one
## chance in three each of north, east and west. Bobby goes on to the
## Clearing; the Woods' three forward exits lead there, and the two wrong
## ones refuse with the source's lost line.

create the Woods
  a room
  aka forest, woodlands, game trails, trails
  north to the Clearing, one-way
  east to the Clearing, one-way
  west to the Clearing, one-way
  north is blocked while the split sapling is not northward: lost-in-the-woods
  east is blocked while the split sapling is not eastward: lost-in-the-woods
  west is blocked while the split sapling is not westward: lost-in-the-woods
  northeast is blocked: trees-too-thick
  northwest is blocked: trees-too-thick
  southeast is blocked: trees-too-thick
  southwest is blocked: trees-too-thick
  up is blocked: no-tree-climbing

  You are in the woodlands just southeast of Lord's Keep, following the game
  trails that wind their way through strands of pale birch and black spruce.
  There is just enough moonlight trickling through the upper branches to see
  your way by. {sapling-north}{sapling-east}{sapling-west}

  after the player entering
    first time
      phrase stream-rocks-wobble
      change the split sapling to eastward when one chance in 3
      change the split sapling to westward when the split sapling is northward and one chance in 2
      stop the player's dawdle
      phrase bobby-shows-sapling-north when the split sapling is northward
      phrase bobby-shows-sapling-east when the split sapling is eastward
      phrase bobby-shows-sapling-west when the split sapling is westward
  end after

## `story.ni:6825`, verbatim — before going north from the Crossing, the
## first time.

define phrase stream-rocks-wobble
  The first couple of rocks are easy enough, but the third one wobbles when
  you put your weight on it. You end up pinwheeling your arms desperately,
  contorting yourself in every direction to avoid a tumble and a thorough
  soaking. Finally, with arms outstretched and lower lip clamped between your
  teeth, you pick your way across the remainder of the stream with all the
  grace of a drunkard on a greased tightrope.

  At the edge of the woods, Bobby is nearly doubled over with silent
  laughter. He claps you on the back. 'You're a good sport, Jack,' he says.
  'C'mon, we're nearly there.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:6885`, three ways — a phrase cannot splice the sapling's way.

define phrase bobby-shows-sapling-north
  Bobby motions you over to where he's standing, pushing back the underbrush.
  'Look here, Jack,' he says. 'These trails go every which way, and it's easy
  to get lost. But if you keep an eye out for this one sapling,' — he points
  to a small tree split halfway down its length — '...you'll find the right
  trail.' He heads off through the trees to the north.
end phrase

define phrase bobby-shows-sapling-east
  Bobby motions you over to where he's standing, pushing back the underbrush.
  'Look here, Jack,' he says. 'These trails go every which way, and it's easy
  to get lost. But if you keep an eye out for this one sapling,' — he points
  to a small tree split halfway down its length — '...you'll find the right
  trail.' He heads off through the trees to the east.
end phrase

define phrase bobby-shows-sapling-west
  Bobby motions you over to where he's standing, pushing back the underbrush.
  'Look here, Jack,' he says. 'These trails go every which way, and it's easy
  to get lost. But if you keep an eye out for this one sapling,' — he points
  to a small tree split halfway down its length — '...you'll find the right
  trail.' He heads off through the trees to the west.
end phrase

define phrase lost-in-the-woods
  If you go wandering off among these trails without Bobby's help, you'll
  never find your way out of the woods.
end phrase

define phrase trees-too-thick
  The trees are too thick in that direction.
end phrase

define phrase no-tree-climbing
  This really isn't the time for tree-climbing.
end phrase

