## `story.ni:7203` — before going north from under the fountain.

define phrase ducking-down-the-tunnel
  Ducking your head to avoid the low ceiling, you follow the tunnel for some
  distance.
end phrase

## `story.ni:7180`, verbatim.

define phrase bobby-finds-the-brick
  Bobby steps past you and brushes his fingertips across the stonework,
  holding the torch close. Eventually he finds a loose brick and pulls it
  free, revealing a rough, rectangular hole.

  You wonder how many times Bobby has been down here. He exudes such easy
  confidence as he reaches into the hole, as though he has done this many
  times before.

  There is a click. Bobby looks over his shoulder at you and winks. And then
  the north wall swings away, revealing shadowed spaces beyond.
end phrase

## `story.ni:7198`, verbatim — the way back.

define phrase we-are-out
  Bobby reaches back and pulls the secret door shut, carefully easing it the
  last few inches so that it doesn't slam. He puts the loose brick back in
  its place, just as before, and tugs his torch free of the bracket where
  he'd left it.

  We're out, you realize with a sense of overwhelming relief. We made it.
  It's like we were never here.

  'Some adventure, eh, Jack?' laughs Bobby. His voice is disturbingly loud
  after the long minutes of terrified silence in the keep. 'Eavesdropping on
  the Lord's own guardsmen! That's a story that'll have the little ones
  wide-eyed at the Maiden House, eh?' He claps you on the back, and you
  manage a smile. It was fun, and you want Bobby to think you had fun, but it
  will be a while before you feel up to something that dangerous again.
end phrase

define phrase ready-to-go-home
  You're more than ready to go home now, and you're not sure you could find
  the loose brick again in any case.
end phrase

define phrase wall-just-blank
  It's just a blank, featureless, stone wall. You know that one of the bricks
  must be loose, but they all look the same to you.
end phrase

