  Bobby shakes his head, and again points north.
end phrase

create the stone stairs
  aka stairs, steps, stone steps, staircase
  scenery, climbable
  in the Upper Bailey

  Steep stone steps climb up to the top of the wall.

  on the player climbing
    refuse when Bobby is leading: bobby-points-north
    move the player to the Lower Bailey
  end on

create the wall torches
  aka torches, torch, fire, flames, flame, torchlight
  scenery, plural
  in the Upper Bailey

  If you get anywhere near those torches, you'll be spotted in no time. Best
  to stay low and in the shadows.

  on the player taking
    refuse torches-spotted
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse torches-spotted
  end on

define phrase torches-spotted
  If you get anywhere near those torches, you'll be spotted in no time. Best
  to stay low and in the shadows.
end phrase

create the view of Toresal
  aka view, city, skyline, shadows in the distance, pinpricks of light, town, lands
  scenery
  in the Upper Bailey

  It's an impressive view, no doubt, but you really don't have time to admire
  it right now.

  on the player examining
    select first-time
      phrase view-first-time
        The city is a pile of shadows in the distance, lit here and there
        with pinpricks of light: a guardsman carrying a torch, perhaps, or
        some night-owl sitting by the window with a lantern. Seeing it makes
        you realize just how absurd is your current predicament: you would be
        safe back in the city, snug in your bunk in Maiden House, or even
        skulking around the alleyways you know so well. Instead, you're
        breaking into Lord's Keep, sneaking along its walls, one slip-up away
        from getting yourself arrested or worse.
    or
      phrase view-afterwards
        It's an impressive view, no doubt, but you really don't have time to
        admire it right now.
    end select
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse city-too-far-away
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse city-too-far-away
  end on

  on the player searching
    refuse city-too-far-away
  end on

define phrase city-too-far-away
  Unfortunately, the city is too far away.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE GUARDHOUSE and the arrow slit (`story.ni:7498-7566`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Guardhouse
  a room
  aka antechamber, narrow antechamber, archway
  north is blocked: are-you-nuts
  south is blocked while the arrow slit is chattering: bobby-motions-you-back
  south is blocked while the arrow slit is half-heard: bobby-motions-you-back

  A narrow antechamber, with an open archway to the south and an
  iron-reinforced door to the north. Arrow slits to either side lend the door
  an important and dangerous air. {bobby-waiting}

  after the player entering while Bobby is leading
    first time
      phrase bobby-taps-his-ear
      move Bobby here
      change Bobby to returning
  end after

## `story.ni:7516`, verbatim.

define phrase bobby-taps-his-ear
  Bobby creeps forward and presses himself against the wall next to one of
  the arrow slits. He hunches closer, peering around the slit's edge. Then he
  looks up at you and smiles. He points at you, points at the arrow slit,
  then taps his ear. Listen, he's telling you.
end phrase

define phrase are-you-nuts
  Bobby waves you off with an incredulous look. Are you NUTS? his eyes seem
  to ask.
end phrase

define phrase bobby-motions-you-back
  Bobby motions you back, pointing at the arrow slit again. There's something
  he obviously wants you to hear.
end phrase

define trait knock-guardhouse
  on the player knocking
    refuse are-you-nuts
  end on
end trait

create the guardhouse door
  aka door, iron door, reinforced door, iron-reinforced door, big door, tough door, wooden door, planks, bands, rivets, lock
  scenery
  knock-guardhouse
  in the Guardhouse

  The door is big and tough-looking, with bands of riveted iron across its
  thick wooden planks.

  on the player opening
    refuse are-you-nuts
  end on

  on the player entering
    refuse are-you-nuts
  end on

## The two rows of overheard conversation (`story.ni:7544-7557`), verbatim,
## then the guards move on. `listen at` is the source's own grammar.

extend action listening
  grammar
    listen at the target

create the arrow slit
  aka slit, slits, arrow slits, holes, hole, rectangular holes, firelight, voices, voice
  scenery
  in the Guardhouse
  states: chattering, half-heard, heard

  The arrow slits are rectangular holes in the wall, about two inches high
  and six inches wide. Someone on the inside could stick the end of a
  crossbow in there and fire at anyone out here, all the while being totally
  protected by the wall. Fortunately, no one is manning the slits right now.

  From here, you can see flickering firelight through the arrow slit, and
  hear voices on the other side.

  on the player listening
    select stopping
      phrase guards-first-row
        You can hear several voices coming from the room on the other side of
        the arrow slit — guards relaxing on break, probably.

        '...and I says, I don't care who hired you, people are tryin' to do
        honest business here, and you're steppin' on toes. Take yer men and
        hit the road, or else you can continue searchin' for yer urchin boy
        in the city jail. And this tough fellow, him so hard with his sword
        and his scars and all, he just starts to cry, blubberin' like a
        frightened scullery maid!'

        'Haw! Yer lyin', so you are!'

        'Goddesses witness it, 'tis the truth! I could hardly credit it
        meself, and I weren't that impressed with the man to begin with. Go
        on, I says, and gave him a boot to the fundament to send him on his
        way.'

        There is general laughter. You glance at Bobby, but he motions you to
        keep listening.
    or
      phrase guards-second-row
        'You seen tomorrow's detail yet?'

        'Yeah, dress-up duty. Polishing helmets so we can look all pretty for
        the lords and ladies coming down to Baron Fussypants' ball.'

        'Not just lords and ladies, I heard. Word has it the Queen herself is
        on the guest list.'

        'Oh, pull the other one.'

        'Goddesses witness it. They say she's comin' down with her daughter
        in tow, that's why Fossville's hostin' the ball in the first place.
        He's tryin' to get in the Queen's good graces, so's he can marry the
        girl.'

        'What, the Queen?'

        'No, you dim. He wants to marry the Princess. Our Lord Fussypants is
        trying to position himself for the throne.'

        'Well, he's welcome to it, long as I'm paid at week's end. Of course,
        I'm not looking forward to spending a night standing at attention for
        a horde of fancy, feathered fops to goggle and gaggle at, but I guess
        it's better than latrine duty...'

        Bobby pulls you away. He's grinning, his eyes wide and his eyebrows
        cocked. Isn't it great?

        But you're too nervous to care. There are who knows how many men of
        the Lord's Guard right on the other side of that door. Someone could
        walk out at any second. You grab Bobby's arm and give it a firm yank
        — It's time to go.
    or
      phrase guards-moved-on
        The guards' conversation has moved on to other things, and Bobby is
        motioning that you should leave.
    end select
    change the arrow slit to heard when the arrow slit is half-heard
    change the arrow slit to half-heard when the arrow slit is chattering
  end on

  on the player searching
    refuse not-so-close
  end on

  on the player entering
    refuse not-so-close
  end on

define phrase not-so-close
  Bobby puts his hand on your chest, holding you back. He shakes his head.
  Not so close. Too dangerous.
end phrase

